(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-arterial-occlusive-aneurysm-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Arterial Occlusive Disease and Aortic Aneurysm Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 70-year-old man with a recent transient ischemic attack has a high-grade carotid stenosis on duplex and is being prepared for intervention. Which study is considered the gold standard?","choices":["Magnetic resonance angiography","Carotid auscultation for a bruit","Digital subtraction angiography","Duplex ultrasonography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Digital subtraction angiography is an invasive angiographic technique considered the gold standard; it is not used for screening but for guiding therapeutic interventions. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation who takes no anticoagulant develops sudden, severe pain in her left leg two hours ago. The leg is cold and pale, pulses are absent below the femoral artery, and she has numbness over the dorsum of the foot. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Critical limb ischemia","Atheroembolism with blue toes","Intermittent claudication","Acute embolic limb ischemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sudden occlusion of a previously patent artery with abrupt severe pain and absent pulses is acute limb ischemia; the heart is the embolic source in 80 to 90% of cases, atrial fibrillation being a classic example. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman who smokes has cramping in her right calf after walking one block that eases after she stands still for several minutes. Her pedal pulses are diminished on the right. Which is the best initial test?","choices":["Magnetic resonance angiography","Ankle-brachial index","Conventional angiography","Computed tomography angiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The ankle-brachial index is a simple bedside test and the important initial test for suspected peripheral artery disease, comparing ankle to arm systolic pressure to grade severity. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has had six days of worsening left leg pain and coolness. The limb has no sensory loss or weakness, and imaging shows an arterial thrombus. Which intervention fits this time frame?","choices":["Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Major amputation","Oral aspirin alone","Supervised exercise program"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intra-arterial catheter-directed thrombolysis, with alteplase as the thrombolytic, is indicated for acute limb ischemia of less than 14 days' duration. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who smoked for 40 years has no symptoms, but a widened, pulsatile aorta is felt on routine abdominal examination. Which is the study of choice for initial screening for an aneurysm?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography","Magnetic resonance imaging","Abdominal ultrasound","Abdominal X-ray"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Abdominal ultrasound is the diagnostic study of choice for initial screening for the presence of an aneurysm and is also used for serial documentation of aneurysm size. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has had progressive ischemic pain in his right leg for three weeks and is found to have an arterial occlusion. Which intervention falls outside its indication for him?","choices":["Arterial bypass procedure","Intravenous heparin","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Surgical thromboembolectomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Intra-arterial catheter-directed thrombolysis is indicated only for acute limb ischemia of less than 14 days' duration; at three weeks he is outside that window. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with no neurologic symptoms has a 90% carotid stenosis on duplex. He has advanced cancer, and his expected survival is about two years. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Medical management","Carotid endarterectomy","Carotid angioplasty with stenting","Endarterectomy after one more duplex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Surgery for an asymptomatic stenosis is advised only above 80% together with a life expectancy over five years. His limited survival means medical therapy: antiplatelets, risk-factor control and smoking cessation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man who smokes has a 4.3 cm asymptomatic thoracic aortic aneurysm and a blood pressure of 158/94. He asks what he can do to help himself. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Quit smoking; control blood pressure","Blood pressure matters only after surgery","Smoking matters only for leg disease","Nothing changes until repair"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of a thoracic aortic aneurysm includes smoking cessation, beta-adrenergic blockers and aggressive blood pressure control, with surveillance imaging for his size. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man collapses with sudden severe back pain. He is pale, his blood pressure is 78/50, and a pulsatile abdominal mass is felt. There is a Grey Turner sign (flank ecchymosis). Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Large-bore access and emergency surgery","Abdominal ultrasound surveillance","Admit for pain control and serial exams","Beta blocker, then elective repair"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm is hemodynamically unstable and a surgical emergency: large-bore intravenous catheters, type and crossmatch, and immediate operation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man has a painful, swollen right groin two days after a cardiac catheterization through the femoral artery. Ultrasound shows a blood-filled cavity outside the arterial wall, communicating with the lumen. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Femoral true aneurysm","Acute limb ischemia","Atheroembolism","Femoral pseudoaneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm is a communication between the arterial lumen and overlying connective tissue after arterial rupture; the common site is the femoral artery after cardiac catheterization, from laceration by the access needle. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with known aortic atherosclerosis has sudden pain and tenderness of two toes, which have turned blue. Which pulse finding is expected on examination?","choices":["A pulse with a double systolic peak","Distal pulses usually remain palpable","Pulses absent below the femoral artery","Pulses absent in both groins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atheroembolic debris tends to lodge in small vessels of muscle and skin and may not occlude large vessels, so distal pulses usually remain palpable, unlike acute limb ischemia. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with no neurologic symptoms and no bruit asks her clinician to order a carotid ultrasound to screen for blockage. Which response is most appropriate?","choices":["Screen with duplex every year","Screen with angiography instead","Screen only if she smokes","Screening is not recommended"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The preventive services task force recommended in 2014, and reaffirmed in 2021, against screening for asymptomatic carotid artery stenosis in the general adult population, as the harms of screening outweigh the benefits. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with ischemic rest pain notices that walking around the room eases his foot pain, although walking used to cause calf pain. Which explanation should he be given?","choices":["Walking releases natural pain relief","Walking drains venous blood away","Walking lowers his blood pressure","Dependency uses gravity to aid flow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rest pain is relieved by hanging the leg over the bed or walking around, a paradox: unlike claudication, walking uses the gravity effect of dependency on extremity perfusion. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with stable calf claudication is starting exercise therapy. She asks whether walking on her own at home is as good as a formal program. Which is the most appropriate recommendation?","choices":["Refer to supervised exercise rehabilitation","Walk at home; supervision adds nothing","Refer for bypass graft surgery now","Avoid exercise until pain resolves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients should be referred to an exercise rehabilitation program; a supervised program is more successful than a home-based one, and consistent moderate exercise improves walking distance. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has claudication after half a block despite six months of supervised exercise, smoking cessation and cilostazol, and can no longer work. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start intravenous heparin","Proceed to amputation","Refer for revascularization","Repeat the exercise program"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Revascularization is recommended for claudication with severe functional disability and an inadequate response to exercise or drug therapy, by endovascular angioplasty with or without stenting, atherectomy, or bypass. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has had painful blue toes for several days after atheroemboli from proximal plaques. Which complication may follow in the affected digits?","choices":["Rupture of the popliteal artery","Hoarseness from nerve stretch","Heart block from nodal fibrosis","Digital necrosis and gangrene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Digital vascular occlusion from atheroemboli causes ischemia and blue toe syndrome, and digital necrosis and gangrene may develop in the affected toes. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has an asymptomatic ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm that now measures 5.9 cm. Repair is planned. Which approach is usually required for this location?","choices":["Watch until it reaches 6.5 cm","Open repair with root replacement","Endovascular repair only","Surveillance every 3 to 5 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Surgery is generally indicated for ascending aneurysms over 5.5 cm, and an ascending aneurysm often requires an open surgical approach with aortic root replacement. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has sudden coolness of her left foot with minimal pain in the toes only. She has no sensory loss and no muscle weakness. Which prognosis applies to this limb?","choices":["Salvageable if promptly treated","Not immediately threatened","Salvageable only with immediate revascularization","Major tissue loss is inevitable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A marginally threatened limb, with minimal toe pain and no sensory loss or weakness, is salvageable if treated promptly, and there is time for vascular imaging first. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has a 1.4 cm femoral pseudoaneurysm on ultrasound after cardiac catheterization. She is comfortable and the limb is well perfused. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Watch and wait","Open surgical repair","Intravenous heparin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm under 2 cm in diameter may undergo spontaneous thrombosis and regression, so watchful waiting is appropriate. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with hypertension has an acute aortic dissection with severe tearing chest pain and a blood pressure of 176/100. Which intravenous medication is appropriate to lower her pressure?","choices":["Esmolol","Alteplase","Heparin","Cilostazol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blood pressure in dissection is reduced with a beta blocker, esmolol being the named example, or a vasodilator such as nitroprusside, to a systolic of 100 to 120 mmHg. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man had a transient ischemic attack last month. Duplex shows 85% stenosis of the ipsilateral internal carotid artery, and he is a good surgical candidate. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Antiplatelet therapy alone","Carotid stenting via the groin","Repeat duplex in one year","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Surgery is advised for symptomatic patients with more than 70% stenosis, and carotid endarterectomy, removal of plaque through a surgical incision, is the gold standard for high-grade carotid stenosis. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man is newly diagnosed with peripheral artery disease after an abnormal ankle-brachial index. He has no chest symptoms. Because half of such patients share it, which associated condition should be evaluated next?","choices":["Pseudoaneurysm of the femoral artery","Venous insufficiency","Coronary artery disease","Aortic dissection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral artery disease shares the pathophysiology of coronary artery disease, and 50% of patients with peripheral artery disease also have coronary artery disease, so it should be sought. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man's asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm measured 4.1 cm six months ago and now measures 4.8 cm. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Wait until it reaches 5.5 cm","Repair the aneurysm","Ultrasound in three years","Recheck in 6 to 12 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Evidence of rapid growth, 0.5 cm in six months, is an indication for repair regardless of size; his aneurysm grew 0.7 cm, and fast expansion also raises rupture risk. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has an asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 4.2 cm, unchanged from last year. Which follow-up is most appropriate?","choices":["Elective repair now","Emergency repair today","Imaging every 6 to 12 months","Ultrasound every three years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Surveillance frequency depends on size: an aneurysm of 4.0 to 5.4 cm is imaged with ultrasound or computed tomography every six to 12 months. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with a 6.1 cm abdominal aortic aneurysm is scheduled for elective repair. He asks how the surgeon will choose between endovascular and open repair. Which factor guides the choice?","choices":["His age and comorbidities","The color of his skin","The result of his ankle index","Whether he has a carotid bruit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two methods exist, endovascular aneurysm repair with a modular graft placed through the iliac or femoral arteries, and open replacement with a tube or bifurcated graft; the choice considers age and medical comorbidities. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man is diagnosed with an acute aortic dissection. His family asks what happens if it is not treated. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Only the legs are at risk","Untreated, mortality is high","It usually heals by itself","It turns into a pseudoaneurysm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute untreated aortic dissection carries a high mortality rate, which is why blood pressure is lowered at once, pain is controlled and surgical grafting of the diseased aorta is pursued. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with long-standing hypertension has sudden, severe, tearing chest pain radiating to his back. Blood pressure is 184/106. Imaging confirms an aortic dissection. Which blood pressure goal applies first?","choices":["Systolic above 140 mmHg","Systolic below 80 mmHg","No change until surgery","Systolic 100 to 120 mmHg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Management stops progression by reducing blood pressure, bringing systolic pressure to 100 to 120 mmHg with a beta blocker such as esmolol or a vasodilator such as nitroprusside. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a 4.8 cm descending thoracic aortic aneurysm, and he now has back pain that is attributed to the aneurysm. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Repair only above 6.0 cm","Repair the aneurysm","Imaging every 6 to 12 months","Imaging every 3 to 5 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Repair is recommended for all symptomatic thoracic aortic aneurysms, whatever the diameter; size thresholds apply only to asymptomatic ones. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has lifestyle-limiting calf claudication despite six months of supervised exercise and antiplatelet therapy, and revascularization is being planned. Which study is the gold standard for defining his arterial anatomy?","choices":["Ankle-brachial index","Pulse volume recording","Conventional angiography","Duplex ultrasound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Conventional angiography is the gold standard, the most accurate and informative test. It is invasive, requiring vascular puncture and contrast at the stenosis, and is used mainly to guide a planned intervention. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man is scheduled for a femoral-popliteal bypass graft using his saphenous vein. He asks what can go wrong. Which complications should he be counseled about?","choices":["Kidney stones and gout flares","Heart attack, stroke, graft infection","Hoarseness and difficulty swallowing","Heart block needing a pacemaker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bypass graft surgery is highly effective, but its complications include myocardial infarction and stroke, infection of the graft, and peripheral embolization. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
